---
import Profile from '../../../layouts/Profile.astro';
import TitleBlock from '../../../components/TitleBlock.astro';
import PairChart from '../../../components/PairChart.astro';
import { person, site } from '../../../data/site';
import { study } from '../../../data/engineering';

const d = study.design;
---

<Profile
  path="engineering"
  title={`Hostel solid waste study · Final-year research · ${person.fullName}`}
  description="B.Eng. final-year research: solid waste generation and characterization in a male and a female hostel at Rivers State University, with recovery potential, heating values and IPCC Tier 1 methane emission estimates."
>
  <header class="wrap head">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/engineering/projects">Projects</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">{study.short}</span>
    </nav>
    <p class="label">{study.type}</p>
    <h1>{study.title}</h1>
    <TitleBlock
      label="Project details"
      fields={[
        { term: 'Type', value: 'B.Eng. final-year project' },
        { term: 'Year', value: study.year },
        { term: 'Study site', value: study.site },
        { term: 'Supervisor', value: study.supervisor },
        { term: 'Hostels', value: 'Hostel E (male) and Hostel D (female)', wide: true },
      ]}
    />
  </header>

  <div class="wrap doc">
    <aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="label">On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#problem">Problem and aims</a></li>
        <li><a href="#method">Method</a></li>
        <li><a href="#generation">Generation and composition</a></li>
        <li><a href="#recovery">Recovery potential</a></li>
        <li><a href="#climate">Climate impact</a></li>
        <li><a href="#recommendations">Recommendations</a></li>
        <li><a href="#role">My role</a></li>
      </ol>
    </aside>

    <article class="body">
      <section id="problem" aria-labelledby="h-problem">
        <h2 id="h-problem"><span class="n">1</span>Problem and aims</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            University hostels concentrate hundreds of people in a small area, and the waste they produce has to go
            somewhere. Planning collection, recycling or composting needs real numbers: how much waste each person
            generates, what it is made of, and what happens if it ends up on an open dump.
          </p>
          <p>The study set out to:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>measure solid waste generation rates in a male and a female hostel and compare them;</li>
            <li>characterise the waste by category and weight;</li>
            <li>assess its potential for composting, recycling and energy recovery;</li>
            <li>estimate methane emissions and global warming potential if it is disposed of by open dumping.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="method" aria-labelledby="h-method">
        <h2 id="h-method"><span class="n">2</span>Method</h2>
        <dl class="spec">
          <div><dt>Rooms sampled</dt><dd>{d.roomsPerHostel} per hostel</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Occupants</dt><dd>{d.occupantsPerHostel} per hostel</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Sampling days</dt><dd>{d.samplingDays}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Waste sorted</dt><dd>{d.totalKg} kg</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Categories</dt><dd>{d.categories}</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Waste was collected from the sampled rooms, hand-sorted into nine categories and weighed on a digital scale
            that was zero-checked every day. Gloves and masks were worn throughout.
          </p>
          <p>
            Generation rates were calculated per person per day. For the energy assessment I applied proximate and
            ultimate analysis and derived empirical formulae using the Tchobanoglous weighted-average method. Methane
            emissions from open dumping and the resulting global warming potential were estimated with the IPCC 2006
            Tier 1 method, alongside biological methane potential.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="generation" aria-labelledby="h-gen">
        <h2 id="h-gen"><span class="n">3</span>Generation and composition</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The female hostel generated about five times more waste per person each day than the male hostel. In both,
            plastics dominated by weight, followed by food waste.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="charts">
          <PairChart
            id="gen"
            title="Waste generation rate"
            caption="Kilograms per person per day"
            unit="kg"
            decimals={3}
            rows={[{ label: 'Generation rate', ...study.generation }]}
          />
          <PairChart
            id="plastics"
            title="Plastics as a share of total waste"
            caption="Percentage by weight"
            unit="%"
            max={100}
            rows={[{ label: 'Plastics', ...study.plastics }]}
          />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="recovery" aria-labelledby="h-rec">
        <h2 id="h-rec"><span class="n">4</span>Recovery potential</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Each category was classed as biodegradable, recyclable and combustible to judge which recovery routes
            make sense. The classes overlap (a plastic bottle is both recyclable and combustible), so the percentages
            do not add up to 100.
          </p>
        </div>
        <PairChart
          id="fractions"
          title="Recoverable fractions by hostel"
          caption="Percentage by weight. Classes overlap and are not additive."
          unit="%"
          max={100}
          rows={study.fractions.map((f) => ({ label: f.label, male: f.male, female: f.female }))}
        />
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            With almost all of the waste combustible, the estimated heating values came out at about{' '}
            <strong>{study.heatingValues[0].toLocaleString('en-GB')}</strong> and{' '}
            <strong>{study.heatingValues[1].toLocaleString('en-GB')} kJ/kg</strong> for the two hostels. These are the
            values that matter when weighing energy recovery against recycling and composting.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="climate" aria-labelledby="h-cli">
        <h2 id="h-cli"><span class="n">5</span>Climate impact</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            If the waste collected during the study were openly dumped, the estimated methane emissions would be
            equivalent to <strong>{study.gwp.combined} kg CO₂</strong> across both hostels. This covers the study
            period only. It is not an annual figure.
          </p>
        </div>
        <PairChart
          id="gwp"
          title="Global warming potential of open-dump methane"
          caption="kg CO₂-equivalent over the study period, IPCC 2006 Tier 1"
          unit="kg CO₂-eq"
          rows={[{ label: 'Global warming potential', male: study.gwp.male, female: study.gwp.female }]}
        />
      </section>

      <section id="recommendations" aria-labelledby="h-recs">
        <h2 id="h-recs"><span class="n">6</span>Recommendations</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>I framed the results within Integrated Solid Waste Management (ISWM) and the waste hierarchy:</p>
        </div>
        <div class="recs">
          <div>
            <h3>University management</h3>
            <p>Segregation bins, scheduled collection, composting and periodic waste audits.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Regulators</h3>
            <p>Managed disposal with gas control, and plastics recycling.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Students</h3>
            <p>Recommendations addressed to students, who generate the waste in the first place.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="role" aria-labelledby="h-role">
        <h2 id="h-role"><span class="n">7</span>My role</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            This was my own final-year project, carried out under the supervision of {study.supervisor}. I designed and
            ran the fieldwork, did the sorting and weighing, carried out the calculations and analysis, and wrote up
            the findings and recommendations.
          </p>
          <p>
            Full details are summarised in my <a href={site.cv.engineering} download>engineering CV (PDF)</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Profile>

<style>
  .head {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-l);
    padding-block: var(--space-2xl) var(--space-xl);
  }

  .crumbs {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .crumbs a {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-4);
    max-width: 18em;
  }

  .doc {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .toc ol {
    padding-left: 1.2em;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.3rem;
    margin-top: var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  .toc a {
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .toc a:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .body {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3xl);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .body > section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-l);
  }

  .body h2 {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    padding-top: var(--space-m);
    border-top: 1.5px solid var(--en-text);
  }

  .n {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--accent);
    transform: translateY(-0.4em);
  }

  .spec {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(8rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-m);
  }

  .spec div {
    padding: var(--space-s) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .spec dt {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .spec dd {
    margin: 0.2rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .charts {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-l);
  }

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .charts {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  .recs {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 13rem), 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-l);
  }

  .recs div {
    padding: var(--space-l);
    background: var(--surface);
    border-top: 3px solid var(--accent);
  }

  .recs h3 {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
  }

  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .doc {
      grid-template-columns: 13rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-3xl);
    }
    .toc {
      position: sticky;
      top: 8rem;
      align-self: start;
    }
  }
</style>
